"settings.help.whatsNewTitle" = "What's New";
"settings.help.whatsNewBody" = "v2.11.0 — Custom words and custom prompts travel to your other Macs over iCloud. Nothing to set up: the same Apple ID is enough. Deliberately left behind are API keys (they stay in the Keychain, which Apple syncs itself), shell snippet approvals (bound to the device — a synced shell snippet asks again on the second Mac, by design), the local model choice and port (hardware-bound), and anything holding a file path. When both Macs changed a value, the newer one wins rather than simply the last one written. Also: the model list now says what the error count refers to (10 test dictations), and the text above it no longer names models that stopped being offered in 2.10.0.\n\nv2.10.0 — Custom words: under Settings → Dictionary & Snippets you list terms transcription keeps spelling wrong — brands, product and proper names, jargon. Tippi enforces exactly your spelling, never inserts a term that was not spoken, and never bends other words to match. Espanso snippets can now be imported instead of only referenced: they live in Tippi's own store and survive path changes. A snippet with a shell command asks once, per snippet, showing the exact command — and that approval is cryptographically bound to that command, so an edit asks again. The local MLX models were measured against real German dictations; the list now shows the result and the real download size instead of adjectives, and the default follows the measurement. A first-time local model download finally shows progress instead of pulling several gigabytes in silence and then reporting a misleading server error. Settings now has a sidebar and can be resized. Security: a local-only provider no longer falls through to the cloud when its server is down — text stays on the machine unless you turn fallback on explicitly. Shell snippets run with a pinned PATH so an approval authorises a binary, not just a string. The old, forgeable file-approval path was removed in favour of import. An unreadable snippet file can no longer overwrite itself. Snippets created by versions up to 2.3.0 are brought up to the current spelling on load instead of going quiet without a word. And typing is smooth again when approved shell snippets exist: verifying them cost a Keychain read on every keystroke.\n\nv2.9.1 — Fixes for the selection action bar and text replacement: the bar could stop appearing for a whole session — of the three features waiting on Accessibility at launch, it was the only one not restarted when the permission arrived. Replacing a selection could append a second copy instead of replacing it, in apps that quietly decline to restore the captured selection. A transform that changes nothing (\"Convert umlauts\" on text without umlauts, lowercase on lowercase text) appended a copy as well, and now reports \"Nothing to change\" instead. The bar also appeared in the bottom-left corner of the screen in Obsidian and other Electron apps, which answer the position query with an all-zero rectangle. The underscore button was invisible and had been since it shipped, because \"underscore\" is not a system icon — every case and separator action now uses a readable label (AA, aa, Aa, A_b, a_b, A b, A-b, \u00e4\u00f6\u00fc). New: split underscores, lowercase + underscores in one click, and the bar hides itself after five seconds unless the pointer is resting on it. Dictation works again in locally built copies.\n\nv2.8.4 — The toast pill (\"Formatting removed\", \"Dictation inserted\", …) could still get stuck on screen even after the v2.7.0 fix — a hard-deadline safety net now force-hides it regardless of whether the fade-out animation's own completion handler fired.\n\nv2.8.3 — Fixed Tippi's own AI/formatting tools not working inside its own Notes editor: selecting text in a note and running a local action (Bold, uppercase, …) or an AI prompt could append the result after the original text instead of replacing it, and the selection popup never appeared over Notes at all. Both now use a direct, native path built specifically for Notes.\n\nv2.8.2 — Fixed choosing a font in Notes silently doing nothing: the \"Aa\" toolbar button now focuses the editor before opening the Font Panel, since macOS only delivers a font change to whatever text view already has focus.\n\nv2.8.1 — Fixed a note appearing twice, most visibly in the new Favorites section: renaming a note's file when it got a title (e.g. via \"Generate title with AI\") could silently fail to delete the old filename, leaving both on disk. Already-affected notes clean themselves up automatically the next time the Notes window opens.\n\nv2.8.0 — Favorite notes: star one from the list row or its context menu, starred notes sort into their own Favorites section. Menu bar polish: every item now has an icon, and the readiness header shows a colored icon and label instead of plain gray text. The Local Quick Actions popup got colored icon badges per category and tighter spacing. Also fixed: the \"Insert Today\" variable could render month names in English instead of German (found in a second code-review pass, same bug already fixed once for the weekday variable), and favorited notes could go stale on a second Mac until relaunch.\n\nv2.7.0 — Notes polish from real feedback: export any note as a plain .txt via a new toolbar button; a stuck-toast bug fixed (a fast second toast could race an earlier one's fade-out and never disappear); the titlebar reverted to a standard Mac titlebar instead of looking oddly transparent; and more breathing room around the text and word/character counter, which sat cramped against the window edges.\n\nv2.6.0 — Three Notes improvements: file names in Finder now show the note's title (renames automatically when it changes) instead of a bare UUID; font and size are choosable via the standard macOS Font Panel (toolbar button); and the window now appears in ⌘Tab while open, with a temporary Dock icon, since Tippi is normally invisible to the app switcher entirely.\n\nv2.5.0 — Liquid Glass on every window: Settings, Welcome, Preview, and Notes now use the same glass material as the floating panels, instead of a solid background — one consistent look across the whole app (macOS 26+, unchanged below that). Plus AI-generated note titles: a sparkles button in the Notes editor asks your configured AI provider for a short title and inserts it above your text, never replacing anything.\n\nv2.4.1 — Notes are now visible in Finder as iCloud Drive → Tippi → Notes, instead of syncing invisibly inside the app's private container. No data moved, same files.\n\nv2.4.0 — Pin the Notes window: a toolbar button keeps it floating above every other app's windows — switch apps (⌘Tab), move to a different Space, or open a full-screen app, and it stays visible instead of getting buried like a normal window. Off by default, remembered across launches.\n\nv2.3.1 — Two fixes: letter-ending emoticons (:o, :O, :p, :P) no longer convert the instant they're typed — they were destroying real snippet triggers like \":ot\" before the snippet check ever got a chance to run, since the emoticon fired first. They now wait for one more non-word character before converting. Also: the \"Insert Variable\" weekday picker's month-name formats now always render in German, instead of whatever locale the shell process happened to inherit.\n\nv2.3.0 — Notes window: press ⌥⌘N (configurable in Settings → Hotkeys, same enable-toggle-plus-remap as Translate and the emoji picker) for a resizable window — a note list on the left, a plain-text editor on the right. Each note is its own plain .txt file, readable directly in Finder, synced across your Macs via iCloud with a local fallback (and automatic migration into iCloud once it becomes available) when you're signed out. Window size, position, and sort order sync too, via a small separate iCloud setting store — never your note content, and never an API key or any other credential. Pasting anything with formatting — an email, a web page, a styled document — lands as clean plain text automatically, with a quiet \"Formatting removed\" toast confirming it. Plus native spell check and a live word/character counter. Deleting a note always asks for confirmation first.\n\nv2.2.0 — Dictation on a single key: pick \"Single modifier key\" in Settings → Voice → Dictation (right Shift by default) and both gestures live on one key — tap it twice to start and stop, or hold it to record only while you keep it down and release to insert. Short dictations no longer need a two-handed combination. The key combination remains the default, so your configured hot key does not change. A hold that never sees a release — a stuck key — stops itself after five minutes instead of recording indefinitely. This style listens to the keyboard directly and therefore needs macOS Input Monitoring; Settings now tells you when it is missing and offers to request it, instead of the key silently doing nothing. Also new: the recording pill shows how long you have been speaking, and can sit at the top of the screen instead of the bottom. On macOS 26 and later every floating surface — pill, cursor popup, selection bar, emoji picker, toast, Translate panel — now uses Liquid Glass; below that they render exactly as before.\n\nv2.1.0 — Emoji without the system palette: New picker at ⌥⌘E — typing filters, arrow keys select, Return inserts, and the emoji lands wherever your cursor was. Search works in German and English (:rakete: as well as :rocket:, \"kino\" finds 🍿), and recently used ones appear first. Plus shortcodes while typing: :rocket: becomes 🚀 instantly, anywhere on your Mac, no hotkey — along with 135 hand-picked everyday shortcuts (:thumbsup: 👍, :heart: ❤️, :thanks: 🙏). Common text emoticons convert too (:-) ;-) :( <3), on their own toggle. If Tippi doesn't know a name your text is left exactly as typed — it never guesses, and timestamps like 12:30: never trigger. Also in the Translate panel: multi-line text is no longer clipped, and a Replace button (⌘⏎) writes the translation straight back over your selection. With this, Tippi covers what usually takes several separate apps running side by side: emoji picking, text shortcuts, a selection bar, dictation, translation and AI text work — one app, one set of permissions, one update path.\n\nv2.0.1 — Critical fix: the v2.0.0 selection action bar could block ⌘C/⌘V/⌘X/Delete/typing/Escape in whatever app you had text selected in — it was becoming the system's one key window despite having no text field that needed one. It never becomes key now, clicks still work immediately, and Escape closes it directly.\n\nv2.0.0 — Text Snippets, auto-popup on text selection, and more: Espanso-style live-typing expansion, now built in — reads existing Espanso match files directly, dynamic date/weekday values via a picker with no shell syntax. New: a PopClip-style action bar that appears automatically next to any text selection (Settings → General, off by default, position configurable with auto-flip). The Translate Quick Panel now pre-fills with your current selection and has full source/target language pickers (German/English/Spanish/French/Japanese) with a swap button, instead of only auto-detecting German⇄Spanish. New \"Convert umlauts\" action, and Underscore/Hyphenate now transliterate umlauts automatically too. Help tab restructured: 7 collapsible categories with a search field instead of one long list. Also fixes a pre-release audit finding: a hanging shell command in a snippet variable could permanently disable snippet expansion — now hard-timed-out.\n\nv1.24.2 — Dictation cleanup no longer cold-starts the local MLX model after transcription: the on-device server already warmed up while you were speaking for the Whisper/Parakeet engine, but not for the AI cleanup step when it was pinned to MLX via a per-prompt override (auto-start at app launch only covered the global default provider). Now both warm up together, so loading overlaps with your recording instead of stalling after it finishes.\n\nv1.24.1 — Small icon polish: two success checkmarks (copy toast + local-model setup) now animate with a gentle bounce instead of appearing static.\n\nv1.24.0 — The update window now reliably comes to the front: as a menu-bar-only app Tippi has no Dock icon to signal a waiting update, so a window sitting behind a large one was simply never seen. It is now actively raised once it exists (the previous attempt failed because Sparkle creates the window only after the signal fires). Afterwards it behaves normally and can be left open. Also: the \"Model may be outdated\" warning now has a \"Switch to <model>\" button — the suggestion is checked against the same live catalogue that reported the problem, so it can never propose another dead model.\n\nv1.23.0 — Model catalogue audited and updated: a full check against every provider's own documentation found four of the nine cloud providers on stale or already-deprecated defaults. OpenAI's entire gpt-4o/gpt-5 line no longer exists (now gpt-5.6 Luna/Terra/Sol), Groq deprecated both Llama models Tippi used in June 2026 (now GPT-OSS), and two Anthropic presets were a generation behind (now Sonnet 5 / Opus 5). Gemini now uses the auto-updating alias \"gemini-flash-latest\" instead of a pinned version — the very property that carried Mistral through every retirement wave untouched. All dead ids are migrated automatically on next launch. Note: Claude Haiku 4.5 remains the fastest, cheapest recommendation, but Anthropic lists its retirement as no sooner than 2026-10-15.\n\nv1.22.1 — Fixed false \"model may be outdated\" warnings: the v1.21.0 check fetched each model list without a page size — Anthropic returns only 20 entries by default (newest first), so a perfectly working claude-haiku-4-5 was reported as retired. It now requests the full catalogue, refuses to warn at all on an incomplete one, and treats alias names (claude-haiku-4-5 vs. claude-haiku-4-5-20251001) as a match. New: a \"Which model for what?\" Help section with concrete recommendations.\n\nv1.22.0 — Language detection fixed + better prompts: on short text Tippi could detect the wrong language and then instruct the AI to stay in it — \"LG Michael\" was read as Polish, \"CINEWEB\" too, and when nothing was detected the prompt literally said \"Stay in .\". Now gated by a confidence threshold with a sensible fallback. Also: Shorten, Summarize, Email reply, Make Formal and Humanize now carry concrete before/after examples, which helps small local models most. New in Help: an explicit privacy note that {clipboard} sends your clipboard contents to your AI provider.\n\nv1.21.1 — The launch-time model check (v1.21.0) now runs at explicit background priority so it never competes with a hotkey press right after launch.\n\nv1.21.0 — OpenRouter + proactive model warning: New 11th provider OpenRouter — one key, 300+ models across every major vendor, model format \"vendor/model\". Tippi also now checks in the background at launch whether your configured model still exists at each provider, flagging it in Settings instead of you finding out via a failed real task (a direct response to the gemini-2.5 incident). The retired-model migration logic (previously Nebius-only) is now generalized and also reaches per-prompt provider overrides.\n\nv1.20.4 — Gemini model IDs updated: Google is retiring the 2.5 generation ahead of its official Oct 2026 shutdown — `gemini-2.5-flash` and `gemini-2.5-flash-lite` started returning \"no longer available to new users\" (HTTP 404). The default and fastest preset now point to `gemini-3.5-flash` / `gemini-3.5-flash-lite`. `gemini-2.5-pro` left unchanged (no reliable 3.x replacement confirmed) — flagged in the provider picker as possibly affected too.\n\nv1.20.3 — Switch provider right at the result: the preview now has a \"Switch provider\" menu in the footer — pick a different provider the moment a result disappoints you, and Tippi reruns immediately. Remembers the choice for that prompt (same storage as the Settings picker) — no detour through Settings needed. Not available for prompt chains, which resolve each step separately.\n\nv1.20.2 — Per-prompt provider override + \"no change\" hint: Settings → Prompts → Built-in now shows a provider picker for each of the 24 prompts instead of just \"read-only\" — useful when a small local model changes too little for a specific prompt (real case: \"Improve\" returned a long business email completely unchanged). If a prompt's result comes back identical to the original, the preview now shows a hint badge instead of letting it look like a normal result.\n\nv1.20.1 — Sharper dictation polish: the optional AI cleanup (Settings → Voice → Dictation) now recognizes filler words per language (German/English/Spanish/French/Japanese) instead of mixing them — words like English \"also\" (meaning \"in addition\") are no longer mistakenly stripped as filler. New: hesitation repeats are removed (\"the the file\" → \"the file\"), and German nouns are reliably capitalized.\n\nv1.20.0 — System audio muting: a new option under Settings → Voice → System Audio mutes your Mac's output for the duration of a recording (dictation, voice command, translate) and restores the exact previous state afterward. Off by default. Also: the built-in \"Improve\" prompt now names concrete edits instead of vague guidance.\n\nFull history: CHANGELOG.md in the project repository.";
"settings.help.howTitle" = "How Tippi works";
"settings.help.howBody" = "Select text in any app, then press your hotkey (default ⌥⌘T). A popup appears near your cursor — pick a prompt, preview the result, and replace or copy.";
"settings.help.instructTitle" = "Give an instruction — type or speak";
"settings.help.instructBody" = "Besides the built-in prompts, you can give Tippi a free-form instruction for the selected text. Trigger Tippi with text selected — an input field sits at the bottom of the popup. Type your instruction (e.g. \"reply to this email politely\", \"translate to Spanish\") and press Return, or tap the mic button and speak it. Tippi follows the instruction literally: transform instructions (translate, summarize, shorten) operate on the text as-is, while reaction instructions (reply, respond) produce an answer. The field auto-focuses so you can type right away; press ↓ to jump back to the prompt list.";
"settings.help.previewTitle" = "Live preview & refine";
"settings.help.previewBody" = "The result streams in token by token, so you see it forming instead of waiting for the whole answer. Once it's ready you can refine it in place: type a follow-up in the \"Refine\" field (e.g. \"shorter\", \"more formal\", \"add a greeting\") and it rewrites the current result — chain as many refinements as you like. Keyboard shortcuts in the preview: Return = Replace, ⌘C = Copy, ⌘Return = Append, ⌘R = Regenerate, Esc = Cancel. If a result hits the model's length limit it's kept but flagged \"Cut off\" so you don't replace a truncated text by accident.";
"settings.help.promptsTitle" = "Custom prompts";
"settings.help.promptsBody" = "Go to the Prompts tab to add your own. Each prompt needs a title, an SF Symbol name, and an instruction for the AI. The selected text is sent automatically — no placeholder needed.";
"settings.help.chainsTitle" = "Prompt chains";
"settings.help.chainsBody" = "In the prompt editor switch the type to Chain to run several prompts in a row. Each step's output feeds the next — e.g. Cleanup → English translates your text after fixing grammar. The preview shows the current step; if a step fails, the last good result stays editable. Try the built-in \"Cleanup → English\".";
"settings.help.localActionsTitle" = "Local quick actions";
"settings.help.localActionsBody" = "Tippi is not PopClip: marking text alone does not open the menu. Select text, then press your hotkey (default ⌥⌘T) or choose Trigger Tippi… from the menu bar.\n\nWhen text is captured, Quick actions appear above the AI prompts. They run locally: rich-text formatting, case changes, underscore/hyphen, brackets, join lines, and counts.\n\nBold/Italic/Underline/Strike use RTF when the app supports it; plain-text apps get Markdown-style fallbacks (**bold**, ~~struck~~). Counts only show a number and do not edit text.\n\nIf you see “No text captured”, grant Accessibility for the Tippi.app you launched (local test builds need a fresh toggle after each make build). TextEdit and Notes work best. Hide Quick actions in Settings → General.";
"settings.help.troubleBody" = "Nothing happens when you only highlight text? Press ⌥⌘T (or ⌃⌥⌘T) after selecting — Tippi does not auto-open like PopClip.\n\nHotkey not firing? Grant Accessibility (System Settings → Privacy & Security, then enable Tippi in the accessibility list (renamed on macOS 26+)). Trigger via menu bar ✏️ → Trigger Tippi…\n\nQuick actions show but text does not change? Re-select text in TextEdit/Notes, trigger again, then tap the action. Local builds: enable Accessibility for build/Build/Products/Release/Tippi.app and restart the app.\n\nNew hotkey not responding? Quit and reopen Tippi.";
"settings.help.variablesTitle" = "Prompt variables";
"settings.help.variablesBody" = "Use {placeholders} in your prompt instructions — Tippi fills them in at trigger time:\n\n{clipboard} — your current clipboard content\n{app_name} — the app you're writing in (e.g. Mail, Safari, Slack)\n{language} — detected language of your selected text (e.g. German)\n{selected_text} — the selected text itself\n\nExample — app-aware tone:\n\"Rewrite for {app_name}. In Slack: casual, max 2 sentences. In Mail: formal.\"\n\nExample — clipboard as style reference (copy a sample text first):\n\"Match the tone of this reference: {clipboard}\"\n\nExample — always stay in the right language:\n\"Improve the text. Stay in {language}. Return only the result.\"\n\n⚠️ Privacy note on {clipboard}: whatever is in your clipboard becomes part of the prompt and is therefore sent to your AI provider. Don't use this placeholder while a password, API key or other sensitive data is copied — or pick a local provider (MLX/Ollama) for such prompts, where nothing leaves your Mac.";
"settings.help.category.gettingStarted" = "Getting Started";
"settings.help.category.automation" = "Prompts & Automation";
"settings.help.category.snippets" = "Text Snippets";
"settings.help.category.providers" = "AI Providers & Models";
"settings.help.category.voice" = "Voice & Dictation";
"settings.help.category.misc" = "History & More";
"settings.help.category.troubleshooting" = "Troubleshooting";
"settings.help.searchPlaceholder" = "Search help";
"settings.help.noResults" = "No results.";
"settings.help.selectionPopupTitle" = "Auto-Popup on Text Selection";
"settings.help.selectionPopupBody" = "Like PopClip: select text anywhere and a small bar with the local quick actions appears right next to it automatically — no hotkey needed.\n\nEnable: Settings → General → \"Auto-popup on text selection\". Off by default, since otherwise it would appear for every text selection system-wide.\n\nPosition is configurable: below, above, right, or left of the selection — if another app (Safari's \"Look Up\", Pages' formatting bar, …) already shows its own popup in the same spot, just move Tippi's out of the way.\n\nClicking an icon applies the action immediately and replaces the selection — the exact same actions as the hotkey popup (uppercase/lowercase, underscore, convert umlauts, brackets, character/word count, …).\n\nSeparated by a divider at the end: the translate icon — opens the Translate Quick Panel with the selection pre-filled instead of replacing it directly.\n\nThe bar dismisses itself when you click elsewhere, press Escape, or run an action.";
"settings.help.customWordsTitle" = "Custom words";
"settings.help.customWordsBody" = "Under “Dictionary & Snippets → Custom words” you add terms transcription keeps getting wrong — brands, product and proper names, jargon. Tippi enforces your spelling wherever the term appears.\n\nImportant: the list never inserts words that were not spoken, and never alters other words to resemble your entries. It only corrects the spelling and capitalisation of the terms you listed.\n\nWhy it is needed: models hear the name correctly but normalise its spelling to whatever looks like an ordinary word — CINEWEB becomes CineWeb. No model can guess a house spelling, it has to be told.\n\nThe list takes effect during AI cleanup of a dictation. Weaker models do not always obey it: measured on 2026-09-15, Gemma 4 E2B and Qwen 3.5 4B honoured it reliably, Qwen 3.5 2B did not.";
"settings.help.espansoImportTitle" = "Importing Espanso snippets";
"settings.help.espansoImportBody" = "Tippi lists the Espanso match files in your folder as import candidates. They only run once you take them over with “Import” — the snippets then live in Tippi's own store, survive a moved file, and no longer need Espanso.

A file sitting in the folder is therefore not enough. Up to version 2.9 Tippi read approved files straight from disk; that path was removed because the approval could be forged. Coming from 2.9, import your files once.

Snippets without shell commands are active immediately. One with a shell command asks once — per snippet, showing the exact command. That approval is cryptographically bound to this command and re-checked before every expansion. If the command changes, the approval is void and Tippi asks again.

Two files may define the same trigger. Only one of them takes effect; the other is marked “Overridden” in the list, and every row names its source file. Delete one of the two entries to decide which applies.";
"settings.help.localModelsTitle" = "Which local model for dictation?";
"settings.help.localModelsBody" = "The MLX models offered in Settings were measured on 2026-09-15 against real German dictations, using Tippi's own cleanup prompt and the same path the app uses in production.\n\n• Gemma 4 E2B (3.6 GB) — 0 findings, 0.52 s. Recommended, and the default.\n• Qwen 3.5 4B (3.1 GB) — 0 findings, 0.89 s.\n• Qwen 3.5 2B (1.7 GB) — smallest download, but 6 findings: left filler words in, kept abandoned clauses, dropped sentence punctuation and ignored the custom-word list. Fine for notes, wrong for anything you send.\n• Qwen 3.5 9B (6.0 GB) — largest model, not measured.\n\nScoring covered filler removal, German noun capitalisation, commas, self-corrections, brand spelling, and whether the model cleaned the text instead of replying to it.\n\nTippi does not download these models itself — the local MLX server fetches them from HuggingFace on first use. Progress is shown in Settings, the download happens once and is cached afterwards.";
"settings.help.snippetsTitle" = "Text Snippets";
"settings.help.snippetsBody" = "Type a shortcut anywhere — Tippi replaces it instantly with the stored text. Like Espanso, but built in.\n\nEnable: Snippets tab → \"Enable text snippets\". Needs Accessibility permission (System Settings → Privacy & Security, then enable Tippi in the accessibility list (renamed on macOS 26+)).\n\nCreate your own snippet — click \"New snippet\", enter a trigger and replacement text, done. Example: \":mlg\" → \"Best regards\\nMichael\".\n\nDynamic snippet (with the current date) — in the editor, click \"Insert Variable\", pick e.g. \"Weekday this week\" → Thursday → format \"DD. Month\". Tippi inserts a placeholder automatically and recalculates the date live every single time — never a frozen date.\n\nExample – the \":nl\" cinema-week snippet: combines three variables into one text — this week's Thursday, Thursday + 6 days, current year. Result: \"Cinema week from Sep 10 to Sep 16, 2026\", automatically current every week, without you ever touching it again.\n\nImporting Espanso files — existing Espanso match files (~/Library/Application Support/espanso/match/) are detected and shown automatically (read-only, the file stays the source of truth). The first time a file loads, Tippi asks for one-time approval, showing exactly what the file contains before anything goes live.\n\n⚠️ If an already-approved file's content changes later, Tippi asks again — a silently altered file can never hijack an existing trigger unnoticed.";
"settings.help.syncTitle" = "What travels to my other Macs?";
"settings.help.syncBody" = "Signed in to several Macs with the same Apple ID, Tippi carries your custom words and custom prompts across automatically. There is nothing to set up.\n\nDeliberately left behind:\n\n• API keys. They live in the Keychain and stay there — Apple syncs the Keychain itself, encrypted. The settings store Tippi uses is not built for secrets.\n• Approvals for snippets that run a shell command. The key signing them is bound to this device. If it travelled, approving a command on one Mac would authorise it everywhere. A synced shell snippet shows \"Approval no longer verifiable\" on the second Mac and asks once — by design.\n• The local model choice and the MLX port. Both depend on that Mac's hardware.\n• Anything holding a file path, such as the watched snippet folder.\n\nChange the same value on two Macs and the newer change wins. Snippets themselves do not travel yet; that needs a merge that loses nothing even when both Macs added something at the same time.";
"settings.help.emojiTitle" = "Inserting emoji";
"settings.help.emojiBody" = "Two ways, depending on whether you already know which emoji you want.\n\n1) Picker (⌥⌘E): Opens a Spotlight-style window. Typing filters, arrow keys select, Return inserts — the emoji lands wherever your cursor was. Recently used ones appear first. The hotkey is configurable under Hotkeys.\n\n2) Shortcodes while typing: Type :rocket: and it becomes 🚀 instantly, anywhere on your Mac, no hotkey needed. Found under Snippets → Emoji.\n\nGerman and English both work: :rakete: as well as :rocket:, and :kino: finds 🍿. Plus 135 hand-picked shortcuts for everyday words — :thumbsup: 👍, :heart: ❤️, :thanks: 🙏, :check: ✅, :idea: 💡.\n\n3) Emoticons: Common text emoticons convert as you type — :-) ;-) :( :D <3 XD and around 25 more. Separate toggle under Snippets → Emoji; when off, the : picker and search remain exactly as they are. Only fires after a space or at the start of a line, so code like a[:(b)] or http:// is left alone.\n\nSuggestion list: As soon as you type :e, a list of matching emoji appears next to your cursor. Space accepts the top one, a click picks any, typing more narrows it, Escape dismisses it. Arrow keys and Return belong to the app you are writing in and deliberately do nothing here — use ⌥⌘E for keyboard browsing. Can be switched off under Snippets → Emoji.\n\nIf Tippi does not know a name, your text is left exactly as typed — it never guesses. A shortcode must contain at least one letter, so timestamps (12:30:) and score lines (10:1:) can never turn into an emoji by accident. Your own snippets take precedence if a trigger collides.\n\nSkin-tone variants are deliberately excluded — they would multiply every person emoji by six and flood search results.";
"settings.help.importExportTitle" = "Share & import prompts";
"settings.help.importExportBody" = "Export your custom prompts as a .tippipack file to share them with teammates or keep a backup.\n\nExport all — click \"Export All\" at the top of the Prompts tab.\nExport one — click the ↑ icon next to any prompt.\nImport — click \"Import\", select a .tippipack file, then choose Merge (keeps your existing prompts) or Replace (replaces them).\n\nImported prompts always get fresh IDs, so they never conflict with existing ones.";
"settings.help.apiTitle" = "AI providers & keys";
"settings.help.apiBody" = "Add your API key in the Providers tab. Tippi supports 11 providers — OpenAI, Anthropic, Gemini, Mistral, Scaleway (EU, Paris), Groq, Kimi/Moonshot, Nebius (EU, Amsterdam) and OpenRouter (300+ models behind one key) as cloud BYOK providers, plus Ollama and MLX (both fully local, free, no key needed). Keys are stored securely in the macOS Keychain.\n\nDefault provider: Tippi tries your chosen default first; if it has no key it falls through to the next configured one. With no explicit default it automatically uses the first provider you've added a key for, so a fresh install never stalls on a cold local model. Optional fallback (Providers tab): if your provider fails (rate limit, server or network error) Tippi can retry the next configured provider — off by default since it sends your text to a second provider.";

"settings.help.linksTitle" = "Links & resources";
"settings.help.linksBody" = "[GitHub repository](https://github.com/miwixyz/Tippi) — public, MIT-licensed, source code, issues, releases.\n\n[Landing page](https://miwixyz.github.io/Tippi/) — bilingual EN/DE, features, screenshots, download.\n\n[One-pager](https://github.com/miwixyz/Tippi/blob/main/docs/ONE-PAGER.md) — for Hacker News, Reddit, sharing with friends; what Tippi is in 60 seconds.\n\n[Changelog](https://github.com/miwixyz/Tippi/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md) — every change, every version.\n\n[Latest release](https://github.com/miwixyz/Tippi/releases/latest) — DMG download, release notes, checksums.";

