"settings.help.notesTitle" = "Notes window";
"settings.help.notesBody" = "Press ⌥⌘N (configurable in Settings → Hotkeys, same enable-toggle-plus-remap as Translate and the emoji picker) to open a resizable Notes window: a note list on the left, a plain-text editor on the right.\n\nPin it: the toolbar pin button keeps the window floating above every other app's windows — switch apps (⌘Tab), move to a different Space, or open a full-screen app, and it stays visible instead of getting buried like a normal window. Off by default; the toggle is remembered across launches.\n\nAI title: the sparkles button in the editor's bottom bar asks your configured AI provider for a short title and inserts it as a new first line above your text — nothing you wrote is replaced.\n\nFont: the textformat button in the toolbar opens the standard macOS Font Panel — choose any installed font and size for the editor.\n\nAppears in ⌘Tab while open: Tippi is normally a menu-bar-only app, invisible to the app switcher entirely — a temporary Dock icon appears while the Notes window is open, and disappears again once you close it.\n\nEach note is its own plain .txt file — no proprietary format, visible in Finder as iCloud Drive → Tippi → Notes, named after its title once it has one. It's stored in Tippi's iCloud container and synced across every Mac signed into the same iCloud account. Without an active iCloud account, notes are kept locally instead (Tippi always works) and migrate into iCloud automatically the first time it becomes available. The list refreshes when the window opens, not continuously — a note just created on another Mac may need a second open to appear if it hadn't finished downloading yet.\n\nWindow size, position, and sort order also sync across your Macs, via a separate small iCloud setting store — never your note content, and never an API key or any other credential.\n\nPaste anything with formatting — an email, a web page, a styled document — and it lands as clean plain text automatically, with a quiet \"Formatting removed\" toast confirming it. No menu, no extra step. The editor also has native spell check and a live word/character counter.\n\nDeleting a note always asks for confirmation first — it's permanent, there's no trash in this version.";
"settings.help.historyTitle" = "History";
"settings.help.historyBody" = "Turn on **Settings → History → Record transformation history** to keep a local log of every AI transformation Tippi performs for you. The history is opt-in and default OFF: nothing is written to disk until you flip the toggle.\n\nWhen on, each entry stores the timestamp, the source app, the prompt title, the provider and model used, and the latency in plaintext (so the list and filters stay fast) — but the actual input and output text are encrypted with AES-GCM (CryptoKit) before they ever touch the database file. The 256-bit encryption key is generated on first use and stored in your macOS Keychain (service `com.tippi.app`, account `history.encryption.key`, `WhenUnlockedThisDeviceOnly`, no iCloud sync).\n\nIn the History tab you can: open any entry to see input and output side-by-side, right-click a row to delete it, export the full decrypted history as JSON or CSV via a standard save panel, or wipe everything with **Delete all**. \"Delete all\" preserves the Keychain key — the next write will reuse it. If you want a full reset, also delete the key from Keychain Access.app, then Tippi generates a fresh one on the next entry.\n\nFiles: the database lives at `~/Library/Application Support/Tippi/history.db`. Because Tippi runs without an App Sandbox (required for cross-app text capture), this path is reachable from Finder — your at-rest file protection is macOS FileVault plus the AES-GCM ciphertext for the content fields.";
